WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.025 --> 00:00:13.688
gloom of sadness and woe of a windless night settled over the men like a blanket.

2
00:00:14.268 --> 00:00:16.910
Odysseus urged his men to row, but their hearts weren't in it.

3
00:00:17.511 --> 00:00:21.534
The men thought they were forsaken, cursed by the gods, and despair had taken hold of each of

4
00:00:21.574 --> 00:00:25.076
them. All through the night Odysseus pleaded and begged for them to row.

5
00:00:25.676 --> 00:00:31.468
Finally as a finger of gray stirred in the east, Odysseus addressed his men in anger, "You believe

6
00:00:31.508 --> 00:00:33.529
we are cursed, so you refuse to row.

7
00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:36.811
But by the grace of some mighty god, we were spared destruction.

8
00:00:37.372 --> 00:00:40.353
Although some gods may want us dead, others want us to live.

9
00:00:40.974 --> 00:00:44.235
None of us know what is written on the endless scroll kept by fate.

10
00:00:44.816 --> 00:00:45.676
A new day is dawning.

11
00:00:46.176 --> 00:00:49.818
Our hearts are heavy, but let us fortify our minds and will our bodies forward.

12
00:00:50.079 --> 00:00:52.120
Drummer boy, give us a lively beat."

13
00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:57.663
As the boy beat a lively rhythm on the drums, Odysseus chanted a poem that brightened his men's

14
00:00:57.703 --> 00:00:59.644
hearts, as much as the sun brightened the day.

15
00:01:00.128 --> 00:01:02.549
By noon, they could see another island on the horizon.

16
00:01:02.990 --> 00:01:07.212
When they reached the shore, they drew lots; half the men would stay with the ship and the

17
00:01:07.252 --> 00:01:08.773
other half would explore the island.

18
00:01:09.513 --> 00:01:14.236
As fate would have it, Odysseus was to stay with the ship, and Eurylocus, the ship's lieutenant, was

19
00:01:14.276 --> 00:01:16.817
to lead twenty-two men in exploration of the island.

20
00:01:17.478 --> 00:01:21.560
Eurylocus and his men headed straight for the center of the island because they hoped to find a

21
00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:22.541
fresh water spring.

22
00:01:23.181 --> 00:01:25.442
Instead they found a cottage made of cut stone.

23
00:01:25.942 --> 00:01:31.245
The house was surrounded by an assortment of wild animals: lions, wolves, bears, and many other creatures.

24
00:01:32.126 --> 00:01:36.227
Eurylocus told the men to approach the animals with caution, but the animals were friendly.

25
00:01:36.648 --> 00:01:40.449
They did not attack the men, but wagged their tails and rubbed against the men like some tamed

26
00:01:40.489 --> 00:01:41.310
dogs or cats.

27
00:01:41.330 --> 00:01:47.552
"The creature that lives within," said Eurylocus to his men, "must have powerful enchantment to tame such wild

28
00:01:47.592 --> 00:01:50.554
beasts." "She is no enchantress," said one of the men.

29
00:01:50.574 --> 00:01:51.874
"She's beautiful."

30
00:01:52.595 --> 00:01:55.176
In the sunlit room, there was a beautiful woman at her loom.

31
00:01:55.624 --> 00:01:58.666
She was singing a merry song, and weaving cloth of dazzling beauty.

32
00:01:59.326 --> 00:02:02.868
Before Eurylocus could caution his men, one of them called out to the woman.

33
00:02:03.429 --> 00:02:04.930
She waved to them and invited them in.

34
00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:10.813
Eurylocus believed that danger often comes in beautiful packaging, and he refused to go into the house with

35
00:02:10.833 --> 00:02:11.633
the rest of his men.

36
00:02:11.654 --> 00:02:15.596
"Welcome. I am Circe," said the beautiful woman to the men.

37
00:02:15.616 --> 00:02:20.038
"Please sit at my table, and have dinner with me, because I rarely have guests upon my island."

38
00:02:20.759 --> 00:02:22.460
The men accepted her invitation eagerly.

39
00:02:23.232 --> 00:02:28.335
The sorceress loaded her table with many good things to eat: cheese, fruit, wine, and bread.

40
00:02:28.996 --> 00:02:30.196
She watched them eat the food.

41
00:02:30.717 --> 00:02:32.758
The men did not know that the food was enchanted.

42
00:02:33.498 --> 00:02:35.900
Soon they forgot about their homes, and even their names.

43
00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:39.822
Then, with a flick of her wand, she turned them all into squealing pigs.

44
00:02:40.723 --> 00:02:44.685
Eurylocus, who was watching from outside, was horrified, and hurried back to Odysseus.

45
00:02:45.505 --> 00:02:49.128
When he reached Odysseus and the rest of the men, he was so dismayed that he could not

46
00:02:49.188 --> 00:02:54.071
speak. Odysseus didn't need to hear words to know that something terrible had happened to his comrades.

47
00:02:54.091 --> 00:02:56.873
"You needn't speak," said Odysseus.

48
00:02:56.893 --> 00:02:58.134
"Just show me the way."

49
00:02:59.014 --> 00:03:03.097
Eurylocus pointed in the direction he had come, and Odysseus grabbed his armor and his sword.

50
00:03:03.637 --> 00:03:07.740
Odysseus had not gone far when suddenly Hermes, disguised as a youthful man, approached him.

51
00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:08.961
"Where do you go?"

52
00:03:09.461 --> 00:03:10.002
asked the youth.

53
00:03:10.022 --> 00:03:14.925
"I don't know," said Odysseus, "but something terrible has happened to my men."

54
00:03:14.945 --> 00:03:19.108
"Men? Ha, I think not," said the youth, laughing.

55
00:03:19.828 --> 00:03:23.791
"If you mean Circe's pigs, nothing terrible will happen to them until they fatten up."

56
00:03:24.692 --> 00:03:26.273
Odysseus eyed the youth more carefully.

57
00:03:26.293 --> 00:03:29.296
"You are not who you seem," said Odysseus.

58
00:03:29.316 --> 00:03:32.759
"This is true," said the youth, "but I can help you.

59
00:03:33.739 --> 00:03:36.442
Take this necklace of herb and wear it beneath your shirt.

60
00:03:37.202 --> 00:03:39.004
It will protect you from Circe's enchantments."

61
00:03:40.385 --> 00:03:43.828
Odysseus took the necklace of herbs, and the youth skipped into the sky and was gone.

62
00:03:45.209 --> 00:03:48.972
When Odysseus reached Circe's house, he called out to her, and she invited him in.

63
00:03:49.644 --> 00:03:53.847
"Please sit at my table, and have dinner with me, because I rarely have guests upon my island,"

64
00:03:54.127 --> 00:03:55.628
she said, smiling wickedly.

65
00:03:56.589 --> 00:03:59.231
Odysseus ate with her, but her spell had no effect.

66
00:03:59.651 --> 00:04:02.893
When she flicked her wand and said, "Off to the pigsty with you!"

67
00:04:03.273 --> 00:04:05.055
Odysseus drew his sword and charged her.

68
00:04:05.615 --> 00:04:09.678
Before he could strike her, she fell to her knees, "Who are you and from where have you

69
00:04:09.738 --> 00:04:11.759
come?" "I am Odysseus.

70
00:04:12.159 --> 00:04:15.101
If you value your life, you will release my men from your enchantment."

71
00:04:16.202 --> 00:04:19.336
"Put back your sword, mighty hero," said Circe.

72
00:04:19.356 --> 00:04:24.520
"First, you cannot kill me; second, your visit was foretold; and third, we are to become friends."

73
00:04:25.241 --> 00:04:29.464
Circe reached up and gently helped Odysseus to put back his sword, and then she took him by

74
00:04:29.504 --> 00:04:31.406
the hand and took him to her room.

75
00:04:32.487 --> 00:04:34.949
Circe found Odysseus sitting the next morning, eating nothing.

76
00:04:34.969 --> 00:04:37.171
"Why do you sulk, noble Odysseus?

77
00:04:37.611 --> 00:04:38.952
I promised to do you no harm!"

78
00:04:38.972 --> 00:04:43.436
"You promised to do me no harm, but my men are still pigs squealing and grunting in your

79
00:04:43.476 --> 00:04:46.398
sty. How can I be happy, when my men are thus condemned?"

80
00:04:46.418 --> 00:04:48.740
"Do your men mean that much to you?"

81
00:04:49.416 --> 00:04:50.056
asked Circe.

82
00:04:50.717 --> 00:04:51.477
Odysseus nodded.

83
00:04:51.497 --> 00:04:53.138
"So be it!

84
00:04:53.498 --> 00:04:54.299
I will release them."

85
00:04:54.799 --> 00:04:59.482
With a quick flick of her golden wand, the squealing and grunting in the pigsty ceased, and soon

86
00:04:59.522 --> 00:05:03.424
the gate to the sty burst open and there stood twenty-two dazed and mud-covered men.

87
00:05:03.444 --> 00:05:06.526
"They are rather unsightly," said Circe.

88
00:05:06.546 --> 00:05:09.347
"I simply won't have them in my house until they have bathed."

89
00:05:10.168 --> 00:05:13.370
Circe clapped five times, and five fairy-like nymphs appeared.

90
00:05:13.390 --> 00:05:17.392
"Wash and oil them," she commanded, and the nymphs did as they were ordered.

91
00:05:18.184 --> 00:05:21.386
Odysseus and his men spent many days enjoying Circe's hospitality.

92
00:05:22.146 --> 00:05:26.929
As the days turned into weeks, and the weeks turned into months, Odysseus finally told Circe of his

93
00:05:26.989 --> 00:05:27.749
desire to leave.

94
00:05:27.769 --> 00:05:32.332
"You have been very kind, but your kindness is just another kind of enchantment.

95
00:05:33.012 --> 00:05:34.233
It is time for us to leave."

96
00:05:34.253 --> 00:05:36.814
"It is foretold," said Circe.

97
00:05:37.234 --> 00:05:38.935
"You will leave, but your journey is not over.

98
00:05:39.355 --> 00:05:42.817
Before you can return home, you must seek the prophet named Tiresias."

99
00:05:42.837 --> 00:05:44.978
"And where can I find this prophet?"

100
00:05:45.579 --> 00:05:46.259
asked Odysseus.

101
00:05:46.279 --> 00:05:49.244
"In the land of the dead," said Circe confidently.

102
00:05:49.264 --> 00:05:52.285
"And how, except by death, do I journey to this land?"

103
00:05:52.825 --> 00:05:53.986
asked Odysseus, dismayed.

104
00:05:54.006 --> 00:05:58.087
"Set your whitest sail, smear ash upon your face, and wait.

105
00:05:58.867 --> 00:06:00.828
The wind of death will take you where you need to go."

106
00:06:01.748 --> 00:06:03.529
Odysseus bowed his head in resignation.

107
00:06:04.049 --> 00:06:07.470
Unlike mortal men, Circe could read the script that fate had written.
